'''3.''' A man of any age who has not been married.
{{smaller block|It was my turquoise: I had it of Leah when I was a ''bachelor'': I would not have given it for a wilderness of monkeys.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., iii. 1.}}}}
'''4†.''' A woman who has not been married.
{{smaller block|<poem>He would keep you
A ''bachelor'' still, by keeping of your portion;
And keep you not alone without a husband,
But in a sickness. ''B. Jonson'', Magnetick Lady, ii. 1.</poem>}}
'''5.''' In London livery companies, a person not yet admitted to the livery.—'''6.''' A local name in the United States of a fish, ''Pomoxis annularis'', of the Mississippi valley; a crappie.

Sometimes incorrectly spelled ''batchelor''.
{{smaller|'''Budge bachelors.''' See ''budge''<sup>2</sup>.—'''Knight bachelor.''' See ''knight''.}}

'''bachelorhood''' (bach′e-lọr-hu̇d), ''n.'' [< ''bachelor'' + ''-hood''.] The state or condition of being a bachelor or unmarried man.
{{smaller block|I can fancy nothing more cruel after a long easy life of ''bachelorhood'' than to have to sit day after day with a dull handsome woman opposite. ''Thackeray'', Newcomes, II. ii.}}
{{smaller block|Keeping in ''bachelorhood'' those least likely to be long-lived.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Study of Sociol., p. 95.}}}}

